# Generated by .erb/scripts/third-party-notices/main.ts - byte-compared by CI, so reformatting them
# would make the check fail with a diff the author cannot reproduce.
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.lock.json

# A verbatim rendering of the Terms of Service source document, and the terms the application is
# licensed under. Reformatting a legal text turns a faithful copy into an approximation of one.
TERMS-OF-SERVICE.md

# The notices renderer's golden fixture: it must equal render()'s output byte for byte.
.erb/scripts/third-party-notices/__fixtures__/golden/

# Verbatim copies of real packages' license files, used to prove what licensee identifies them as.
# Prettier DOES reformat the .markdown one (jszip ships its dual MIT/GPLv3 grant as Markdown, and
# prettier rewrites its setext headings and emphasis) - which silently turns a faithful copy of a
# legal text into an approximation of one, in the fixtures a licensing tool's tests rest on.
.erb/scripts/third-party-notices/__fixtures__/licenses/

# Ruby gems, installed into the workspace by CI's ruby/setup-ruby step (`bundler-cache: true` sets
# bundler's path to vendor/bundle) and by any local `bundle install` configured the same way. Third-
# party source that this repository does not author, format or lint - and prettier reports a syntax
# error on the HTML fixtures some gems ship.
/.bundle/
/vendor/bundle/

# Licence texts this repository holds verbatim on behalf of packages that publish theirs at a URL
# and bundle no copy (see `notices-policy.json` `licenseTexts`). They are hash-pinned and
# reproduced byte for byte into THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md, so reformatting one would change a legal
# claim and fail the pin.
.erb/scripts/third-party-notices/vendored-texts/
